Note = {Symposium on the Occasion of the VII International Congress of Ecology
   (INTECOL), FLORENCE, ITALY, JUL 19-25, 1998},
Organization = {Soc Human Ecol; Japanese \& Italian Soc Human Ecol},
Abstract = {Nutritional evaluation of peoples in diversified local ecosystems is one
   of the main foci in a human ecological approach to understanding their
   adaptation. For this purpose, measurements of nutritional indicators in
   biological samples play a key role. Blood samples were collected from
   ten populations living in traditional, modernising, and urban societies
   of Papua New Guinea. Based on the analysis of biochemical parameters,
   special attention was paid to trace element nutrition which is
   particularly related to human health. Among the traditional societies,
   the more traditional lifestyle, the higher zinc (Zn) intake, and
   inter-population variation of Zn intake was parallel to that of the
   serum Zn concentration. Even in the populations with higher Zn intakes,
   however, the serum levels were low compared to developed countries. This
   discrepancy was explained by two factors: high dependency on plant foods
   containing low bioavailable Zn and increased Zn requirement to cope with
   malaria infection. Iron (Fe) was analysed in relation to anaemia which
   has been an important world-wide health problem. The proportion of
   anaemia varied widely among the populations; this difference was
   attributed to Fe nutrition and malaria prevalence. Iron nutrition was
   sufficient in most traditional populations but tended to be degraded in
   most urbanised populations. From these results, it is concluded that
   ecological conditions of each population play decisive roles in their
   trace element nutrition, and adverse effects of rapid modernisation need
   careful evaluation.},
