Organization = {Natsionalen institut po geofizika geodezia i geografia B'lgarska
   akademia na naukite},
Abstract = {Black carbon (BC) is a major component of particulate matter (PM) while
   Polycyclic Aromatic Hydrocarbons (PAHs) are trace elements with
   potential carcinogenic effect. Understanding the association between BC
   and PAHs is critical in order to find out their negative effects,
   transport and fate throughout the environment. This work represents
   comparative study focusing on the relationships and correlations between
   PAHs and BC in air particulate matter fraction PM2.5 estimated in two
   Bulgarian cities. The PM2.5 sampling is performed in October 2020,
   January-February 2021 and July 2021, at the Central Meteorological
   Observatory (CMO), NIMH, Sofia and at the University ``Prof. Dr. Assen
   Zlatarov{''}, Burgas, in the frame of project ``CARBOAEROSOL{''}. The
   analysis of 15 PAHs was performed by extraction followed by GC-MS/MS in
   the selected reaction monitoring (SRM) mode. The Multi-wavelength
   Absorption Black instrument, measuring light absorption at seven
   different wavelengths is used for estimation of black carbon
   concentrations in PM2.5 samples. This instrument measures light
   absorption at seven different wavelengths, spanning from ultraviolet to
   infrared (405 nm, 465 nm, 525 nm, 639 nm, 870 nm, 940 nm and 1050 nm),
   which provides opportunities to differentiate the contributions from
   sources such as biomass burning (BrC) and fossil fuel burning (BC). The
   obtained results reveal that PAHs and BC concentrations in Sofia are
   higher than those in Burgas.},
