Number = {1},
Pages = {127-152},
Month = {FEB},
Note = {TENNET IV Conference, at the Symposium on Inhibitory Mechanisms in
   Theoretical and Experimental Neuropsychology, MONTREAL, CANADA, MAY
   12-14, 1993},
Abstract = {Findings from many areas of psychology and neuroscience indicate that
   inhibitory mechanisms may contribute to relationships between numerous
   causal variables and an equally diverse set of outcome variables.
   Outcome variables that implicate inhibition include measures from
   physiology, perception, attention, action, learning and episodic memory,
   semantic memory, emotion, and psychopathology. Inhibition-related causal
   variables include childhood development and aging, hypoxia and other
   brain insults, and socialization. If confirmed by further research, a
   theoretical framework with inhibition as one of its core mediating
   constructs has several strengths, including unified explanations for
   diverse relationships, mechanistic models for phenomena, insight into
   the principles that underlie observed relationships, and mechanistic
   translations of existing abstract, theoretical constructs. (C) 1996
   Academic Press, Inc.},
