   04, 1994},
Organization = {Amer Assoc Marriage \& Family Therapy},
Abstract = {The goal of this research was to develop an instrument, the Supervisory
   Styles Index (SSI), that would examine supervisory style. Style has been
   described as the way in which the personality and convictions of the
   supervisor are demonstrated in the supervisory relationship (Leighton,
   1991). This study focuses on the interplay between supervisory style and
   the influence of sex and gender. The findings of this study reveal that
   female supervisees discern their supervisors to be more self-disclosing
   than do male supervisees. Implications of the findings and utilization
   of the index are discussed.},
